{:edb/db-before-t 1344 :edb/db-after-t 1345 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534657 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:25:15.476Z" 13194139534657 true] [17592186087460 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x -  6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139534657 true] [17592186087460 1100 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/7a5d03c5fc0581217a488b76e6d39486.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186087460 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4x - 6y &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3 \\cdot 0^{2} - 4x - 6 \\cdot 0 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 2 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 4x + 4) - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 6 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± \\sqrt{6} \\\\\nx &= 2 ± \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0)$." 13194139534657 false] [17592186087460 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x-6y & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-4x-6\\cdot 0 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x+4)-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0).$" 13194139534657 true] [17592186087461 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((0 - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/c435c838bf3a88b169499c58834c75c4.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186087461 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186087465 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((0 - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 12 \\\\\ny - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{12} \\\\\ny - 3 &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ny &= 3 ± 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/686d5b60b8ea3f16af57af3ed5ef341d.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186087465 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ y-3 & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186087466 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20x - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20 \\cdot 0 - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2y^{2} - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y^{2} - 4y) + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[y^{2} - 4y + 4 - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y - 2)^{2} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\n, there is no $y$ such that\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/dadec72b5f61bde4c28bdf1079eb4729.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186087466 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20x-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot {0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20\\cdot 0-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2({y}^{2}-4y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-4y+4-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y-2)^{2}-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{(y-2)}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-2)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186087661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301761 13194139534657 true] [17592186087661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301770 13194139534657 true] [17592186087661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301776 13194139534657 true] [17592186087661 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301816 13194139534657 true] [17592186087662 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301809 13194139534657 true] [17592186087665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301747 13194139534657 true] [17592186087665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301795 13194139534657 true] [17592186087665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301802 13194139534657 true] [17592186087665 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301823 13194139534657 true] [17592186087666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301754 13194139534657 true] [17592186087666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301786 13194139534657 true] [17592186087666 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301830 13194139534657 true] [17592186087986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534657 true] [17592186087986 1100 "Determine the points where the ellipse $((x - 2)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 6)^{2})/(36) = 1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139534657 false] [17592186087986 1100 "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139534657 true] [17592186087986 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186088398 1100 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $((x + 4)^{2})/(3) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(6) = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139534657 false] [17592186088398 1100 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139534657 true] [17592186088398 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186088398 1145 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186088400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534657 true] [17592186088400 1100 "Determine the point where the ellipse $x^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4\\sqrt{3}x - 6\\sqrt{5}y + 15 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139534657 false] [17592186088400 1100 "Determine the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139534657 true] [17592186088400 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}x-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4\\sqrt{3}(0)-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{5}y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{5}y]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3[(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-5]+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2}-15+15 & =0 \\\\ 3(y-\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d37e546bf2b17c99c87650e7c0973a3a881c8c5f3a8ed28f5f923d9d3d500511.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186267931 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)" 13194139534657 true] [17592186267931 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)" 13194139534657 false] [17592186267932 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301775 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301748 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1089 "q-16683" 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1100 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1113 #uuid "73127ff2-1cda-57f0-ae1f-5bfe9c540720" 13194139534657 true] [17592186301747 1145 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =9\\cdot 16 \\\\ 81